athing, disgust, fastidiousness”). A connection to Proto-Germanic *aikulaz (“fearful”) has been suggested for both words; compare Old English acol (“burdensome, troublesome”), English ache, Galician extern (“to vex”), Dutch akelig (“terrible”).\nHeikel seems to have arisen out of 16th century Upper German dialects with a first meaning “easily disgusted, choosy”. Dialectal variants include Bavarian haglich and hoaglich (both akin to regional German heikelig or heiklig), Swabian haikel, Alemannic German heikχel, Saterland Frisian hekel (“fastidious with regard to food”).\nOthers have suggested a derivation from Middle High German heien, heigen (“to cherish, harbour”) (compare Bavarian haigeln). The connection with the root of English irk (“to irritate, annoy, bother”), advanced by the Grimms, seems to be out of question today, even given the Upper German verb erkeln (“to loathe”)."]],"senses":[[29137,"heikel","adj","delicate (requiring careful handling)",0],[29138,"heikel","adj","picky, choosy",1,null,["Austria"]]]},"hinausgeht":{"entries":[["hinausgeht",null,null,null]],"senses":[[657286,"hinausgeht","verb","inflection of hinausgehen:",0],[657287,"hinausgeht","verb","second-person plural dependent present",1],[657288,"hinausgeht","verb","third-person singular dependent present",2]],"inflections":[["hinausgeht","hinausgehen","indicative,plural,present,second-person,subordinate-clause"]]},"hinterließest":{"entries":[["hinterließest",null,null,null]],"senses":[[385697,"hinterließest","verb","second-person singular subjunctive II of hinterlassen",0]],"inflections":[["hinterließest","hinterlassen","indicative,preterite,second-person,singular"]]},"irreguläre":{"entries":[["irreguläre",null,null,null]],"senses":[[297692,"irreguläre","adj","inflection of irregulär:",0],[297693,"irreguläre","adj","strong/mixed nominative/accusative feminine singular",1],[297694,"irreguläre","adj","strong nominative/accusative plural",2],[297695,"irreguläre","adj","weak nominative all-gender s